00:15:44.329 --> 00:15:48.758
but they've never got the disease and that, is kind of what we're praying for because some people

00:15:49.309 --> 00:15:51.459
won't survive this disease and if we can stop

00:15:52.100 --> 00:15:55.389
at least like maybe 10% of the most vulnerable from getting it, that would be great.

00:15:55.509 --> 00:16:00.248
You see why reducing the transmission rate doesn't just squash the curve it also reduces

00:16:00.249 --> 00:16:02.844
the number people actually getting the disease in the long run

00:16:02.844 --> 00:16:08.240
- And of course a vaccine may be developed  in the interim in which case the susceptible people can be made susceptible...

00:16:08.360 --> 00:16:12.240
- Absolutely, you can take them out of the equation entirely if you vaccinate them.

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:17.040
I mean you can also increase the recovery rate if you crank up the way the people recover

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:20.280
you can see that increasing that flattens the curve as well

00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:22.540
but that's much harder to do. The NHS is doing that,

00:16:22.540 --> 00:16:24.939
they're trying to keep people healthy and recover quickly in hospital

00:16:25.340 --> 00:16:30.340
But in practice we know that it takes at least a week or so to get over this thing and sometimes a lot longer

00:16:30.460 --> 00:16:34.980
- There aren't treatments or cures that can really help us move that slider very much.

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:37.600
- Exactly, yeah. We'll do our level best obviously in the NHS

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:42.040
but we can't control it very well and what you might have heard some people talking about on the

00:16:42.559 --> 00:16:46.389
sort of chat shows about modelling this stuff is something called R_0

