00:18:00.280 --> 00:18:06.540
- How do the modellers go change that number based on things like "We're going to close the cinemas",

00:18:06.540 --> 00:18:11.620
"We're going to close the schools, We're going to stop people, we're going to keep people in their house for two weeks or three weeks."

00:18:11.620 --> 00:18:16.900
How do those real-world interactions turn into a number?

00:18:16.900 --> 00:18:21.240
Well, this is where there's a lot of argument out there in the press right? This is guesswork.

00:18:21.250 --> 00:18:26.080
We don't know what the transmission rate and recovery times are, they're not written in the back of a textbook.

00:18:26.420 --> 00:18:29.259
All we can do is estimate them from the data we get

00:18:29.570 --> 00:18:33.159
from say, China, who've been dealing with it longer than the rest of the world,

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:39.020
which is why the data from China is super valuable and also why we need to know whether we can trust it.

00:18:39.500 --> 00:18:43.460
So you go and get data from China and then maybe Italy and then maybe Iran

00:18:43.460 --> 00:18:48.440
and then you ask serious questions about whether that data is reliable, to build your models on.

00:18:49.220 --> 00:18:54.610
And then you start saying "Well if we social isolate maybe we can reduce this by a certain amount", and you have to look at other

00:18:54.610 --> 00:18:59.559
studies that have been done to see how much certain actions will reduce that. But it is guesswork,

00:18:59.560 --> 00:19:04.620
this is why having a model is super important because you can't do the guesswork live,

00:19:04.620 --> 00:19:09.340
and wait to see if everyone dies because that's just not how things should run.

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:15.580
- One thing that was absolutely clear though is no matter what happened, whenever you slid the transmission down,

00:19:15.860 --> 00:19:20.000
the red bump flattened. 
