- Ben, as a math guy, as you've been following this pandemic as it unfolds

00:20:27.580 --> 00:20:30.740
and the way it's been covered in the media and social media and things like that -

00:20:30.890 --> 00:20:37.390
how have you felt about the way that lay people are grasping the mathematics? And how well do you think it's being explained?

00:20:37.390 --> 00:20:42.420
- I think it's hard to explain well, because like all maths it needs a bit of a run-up.

00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:45.000
Even in this description I've been talking about differential equations.

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:49.150
If you're not sure what a rate of change or what differentiation is,

00:20:49.150 --> 00:20:53.200
then that run-up needs to happen to make you comfortable with the rest of the explanation.

00:20:53.320 --> 00:20:58.000
But the number of people who have been talking about exponential growth, logarithmic scales

00:20:58.300 --> 00:21:04.800
which most Numberphile viewers will be familiar with, it turns out they're desperately important to discuss the sort of epidemics that we're seeing.

00:21:05.620 --> 00:21:11.460
So it's been fascinating and sobering to realise sometimes in our mathematical world

00:21:11.520 --> 00:21:15.360
we think we live in these abstract things, but this is desperately important in a very real-life way.

00:21:15.360 --> 00:21:19.260
So that's both exciting for me as a maths educator, like people realise

00:21:19.260 --> 00:21:25.560
this is important, and sobering because it does seem to be the crises when we notice that it's important.

00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:29.620
